  "text": " Thanks for coming, Lauren Jonah. I read your notes from the Mayerford first year ensemble session. Let's review what happened before we plan the second trial. We tested the rehearsal feedback cards with 24 students, after listening to a short performance. Each student chose one of three headings and wrote a brief observation. The headings were timing balance and entries. We explained that balance meant whether every musical part could be heard clearly while an entry was the moment a player began after a pause. And what did you expect the cards to produce? We expected comments to be spread fairly evenly across all three headings. But 18 of the 24 cards only said that a section should play louder or more quietly, while you were simply the easiest feature for listeners to notice. It wasn't a timing problem. Everyone finished within the minutes we allowed, and the students wrote the comments themselves rather than relying on the tutor. So the trial showed that the original cards encouraged attention to one obvious feature instead of a wider range of musical details. How did the colour system work? We printed timing on blue cards, balance on green cards and entries on amber cards. The print stayed clear under the rehearsal room lights, and the card bundles were easy to find on every table. The difficulty was the meaning students gave the colours. Several treated green as a complement and amber as a warning, as if the cards were judging whether the performance was good or bad. Then remove the colours. Use plain black headings and add a one line question under each heading so that students focus on the topic rather than inventing a rating system. When do you intend to collect the next set of comments? My first thought was an online form completed the following day. I wondered whether we should collect everything at the end of the full rehearsal, which lasts about 50 minutes. Both options leave too much time between listening and responding. After each four minute performance, allow 90 seconds for students to complete one card. Collect the cards before anyone discusses the performance. That should keep the comments connected to what they have just heard. What exactly will you compare in the second trial? We considered comparing comments from different groups of instruments. Another possibility was to compare anonymous cards with cards carrying the writer's name. Either choice would introduce any variable. Have the ensemble performed the same short passage twice? Discuss the first set of cards between performances, then compare whether the same musical issue is still present in the second performance. So the comparison is between the first and second versions of one passage, not between different students or instruments? Exactly. What evidence should be central to your report? The total number of cards returned will be easy to count. We'd also planned when question asking whether students enjoyed the activity. Keep those as supporting information. Your main evidence should show whether the problem identified after the first performance is reduced in the repeated performance. For example, if several cards say that players begin at different moments, we should check whether they begin more closely together the second time. Correct. Now let's assign the remaining work. First, the category headings needs simpler wording. I can shorten them, although Lara has already tried a few alternatives. I drafted questions such as do the players begin together, and can every part be heard? I'm happy to finish that work. Given that preparation, Lara should take the headings forward and turn those drafts into the final set. Next, we need two short passages that can each be performed twice without taking too much rehearsal time. I don't have the complete rehearsal score. I do, and I marked three possible passages yesterday. I can select two and time them before Friday. Then Jonah, bring me two timed extracts by Friday. The consent wording also needs checking because the students' comments will be used in your course report. Can one of us approve that? No, it has to be checked by the course tutor. I'll review the wording and confirm that no student names are collected. So that remains your task, Dr Harlow. Thus, who will manage the cards during the next rehearsal? I'll be playing in the demonstration group, so I can't move around collecting them. I'm not performing in that session. I can put the cards on the tables, collect them after each performance, and keep the two sets separate. Jonah, you'll handle the rehearsal materials and keep both rounds apart. Finally, we need one summary visual for the report. I'll already be timing the passages and organising the cards. I can design the visual. I'll show the first and second performances side by side, using the same categories for both. Excellent. La take responsibility for presenting their comparison visually. Bring the revised cards and passages to me on Friday, and we'll confirm whether everything is ready.",
